@startuml net-internals
!theme plain
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam nodesep 20
skinparam ranksep 50
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

title starling-net — five parts, one of them visible

header
<b>net is the only component that touches a socket, and the only one that encrypts.</b>
Everything else sees a frame and cannot tell whether it travelled as plaintext, OCB2 or XChaCha20.
endheader

component "**starling-bus**" as bus #FFE9D6

package "starling-net" as NET #E6F0FA {
  component "**event loop**  «reactor»\nthe one place that awaits socket readiness,\nand **the only part the bus can see**" as loop #FFFFFF
  component "**listener**\nTCP accept · TLS handshake\nmints the connection id" as listener #FFFFFF
  component "**connection**  [0..*]\none TLS stream · control framing\n«type u16 + len u32»\n—\n**two bounded queues, two policies:**\ncontrol full -> **disconnect**\naudio full -> **drop oldest, count**" as conn #FFFFFF
  component "**udp socket**\none shared datagram socket,\ndemuxed to a connection id\nby source address" as udp #FFFFFF
  component "**cipher**  «trait»\nOCB2 · XChaCha20 · plaintext,\nchosen per connection" as cipher #FFF0F0
}

loop <..> bus : frames, **opaque**

loop --> listener : accept readiness
loop --> conn     : read · write readiness
loop --> udp      : read · write readiness
listener *-- conn : on accept

udp --> cipher : seal · open
note right of cipher #FFF0F0
  **Datagrams only.**
  Audio arriving over **UDPTunnel** is already inside
  TLS and must be passed on **plain**. Sealing it a
  second time gives a client that hears silence, with
  nothing in any log to say why — this has already
  cost one debugging session.
end note

note bottom of loop
  **Why the loop is separate from the wire format.**
  "Await readiness and pump the bus" and "speak Mumble's
  framing" are two jobs. Fused, they become one file that
  nobody can review — which is the shape net had before.
end note

note bottom of conn
  **Fan-out lives here.** Voice publishes one frame plus a
  recipient set; net decides per recipient whether that
  means a datagram or a tunnelled TCP frame, because only
  net knows who has a working UDP path.
end note

note left of conn #E8F5E9
  **Why the two policies differ.** Dropping a control
  message desyncs that client permanently and
  silently — it renders the wrong world forever with
  nothing in any log. Queueing without a bound is a
  memory DoS. Disconnecting is the only outcome that
  is both bounded and honest, and reconnect already
  re-syncs from scratch.
  A late **audio** frame, by contrast, is worthless —
  so drop it, and count what was dropped.
end note

legend bottom
| solid, one arrowhead | a direct call inside net |
| dashed, two arrowheads | bus traffic |
| filled diamond | creates and owns |
endlegend

@enduml
